    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Reproductive behaviour</span> refers to the set of actions animals perform to attract mates, secure fertilization, and raise offspring successfully. These behaviours have evolved because they increase the chances of a species' survival.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Courtship Behaviour</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Courtship</span> is the set of behaviours an animal displays to attract a mate and signal readiness to reproduce — for example, birds singing or displaying colourful plumage, or peacocks fanning their tail feathers. Courtship helps individuals select healthy, suitable mates and reduces the chance of interbreeding between different species.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Mating Systems</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Monogamy</td><td>One male mates with one female, often for a season or life</td><td>Many bird species, e.g., swans</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Polygamy</td><td>One individual mates with multiple partners of the opposite sex</td><td>Lions (polygyny), some insects</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Promiscuity</td><td>Both males and females mate with multiple partners with no lasting bond</td><td>Chimpanzees, many fish species</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Parental Care</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Parental care</span> is the investment parents make in protecting and nurturing offspring after fertilization. It varies greatly across animal groups:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Fishes:</strong> Many show little or no parental care (external fertilization, large numbers of eggs); some species like tilapia mouth-brood their young</li>\n  <li><strong>Amphibians:</strong> Some frogs guard eggs or carry tadpoles; most rely on producing many eggs instead</li>\n  <li><strong>Birds:</strong> High parental care — incubating eggs, feeding, and protecting chicks until they can fly</li>\n  <li><strong>Mammals:</strong> Highest parental care — internal development, nursing with milk, and prolonged protection</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Reproductive Behaviours</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">These behaviours increase the survival chances of offspring, ensure successful fertilization, maintain species identity by preventing cross-breeding, and help pass on favourable genetic traits to the next generation.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Don't confuse \"polygamy\" (one individual with multiple mates) with \"promiscuity\" (both sexes mating with multiple partners with no fixed bond) — WAEC often tests this distinction directly.\n</div>\n",
