    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Heredity</span> is the passing of characteristics (traits) from parents to offspring. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Genetics</span> is the branch of biology that studies how these traits are inherited, largely founded on the pioneering work of Gregor Mendel.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Gregor Mendel and the History of Genetics</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Gregor Mendel, an Austrian monk, conducted breeding experiments on pea plants in the 1860s, carefully tracking traits like seed shape and flower colour across generations. His work established the basic rules of inheritance, though it was not widely recognised until decades later.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Key Genetics Terminology</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Gene</td><td>A unit of heredity that codes for a particular characteristic, located on a chromosome</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Allele</td><td>An alternative form of a gene (e.g., one allele for tall, one for short)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chromosome</td><td>A thread-like structure in the nucleus made of DNA that carries genes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Genotype</td><td>The genetic makeup of an organism (its combination of alleles, e.g., Tt)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Phenotype</td><td>The observable physical characteristics resulting from the genotype (e.g., tall plant)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Homozygous</td><td>Having two identical alleles for a trait (e.g., TT or tt)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Heterozygous</td><td>Having two different alleles for a trait (e.g., Tt)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Mendel's Laws of Inheritance</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Law of Segregation:</strong> Each organism carries two alleles for a trait, which separate during gamete formation so each gamete carries only one allele</li>\n  <li><strong>Law of Independent Assortment:</strong> Alleles of different genes are distributed to gametes independently of one another (for genes on different chromosomes)</li>\n  <li><strong>Law of Dominance:</strong> When two different alleles are present (heterozygous), one (the dominant allele) masks the expression of the other (the recessive allele)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Example: Flower Colour in Pea Plants</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">If purple flower colour (P) is dominant over white (p), a plant with genotype Pp will have a purple phenotype, since the dominant P allele masks the recessive p allele. Only pp plants show the white phenotype.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: genotype is the genetic code (letters like Tt), while phenotype is what you actually observe (e.g., \"tall plant\"). Many WAEC questions test whether you can correctly link a genotype to its resulting phenotype.\n</div>\n",
