    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Building on basic genetics terms, this topic covers <span class=\"learn-keyword\">monohybrid and dihybrid crosses</span>, the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Punnett square</span> tool for predicting offspring, human sex determination, and sex-linked traits.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Monohybrid Cross</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">monohybrid cross</span> studies the inheritance of a single trait. Example: crossing a homozygous tall plant (TT) with a homozygous short plant (tt) produces all Tt (tall) offspring in the first generation (F1). Crossing two Tt plants (F1 x F1) produces a 3:1 ratio of tall to short in the F2 generation.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Dihybrid Cross</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">dihybrid cross</span> studies the inheritance of two traits simultaneously, such as seed shape and seed colour. When two heterozygous individuals (e.g., RrYy x RrYy) are crossed, the classic phenotypic ratio in the offspring is 9:3:3:1.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Using the Punnett Square</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Punnett square</span> is a grid used to predict the possible genotypes and their probabilities in offspring, by combining the possible gametes from each parent along the top and side of the grid.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th></th><th>T</th><th>t</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>T</td><td>TT</td><td>Tt</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>t</td><td>Tt</td><td>tt</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">This example (Tt x Tt cross) predicts a genotype ratio of 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, giving a phenotype ratio of 3 tall : 1 short.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Sex Determination in Humans</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes; one pair determines sex. Females have XX; males have XY. Since the mother always contributes an X, the father's contribution (X or Y) determines the offspring's sex.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Sex-Linked Traits</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Some traits are carried on the X chromosome and are called <span class=\"learn-keyword\">sex-linked traits</span>. Examples include haemophilia (a blood-clotting disorder) and colour blindness. Because males have only one X chromosome, they are more frequently affected by X-linked recessive conditions than females, who need two copies of the recessive allele to be affected.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Importance of Genetics</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Genetics is applied in agriculture (breeding higher-yielding crops and livestock) and medicine (understanding inherited disorders, genetic counselling, and blood transfusion compatibility).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Memorise the standard ratios: monohybrid F2 cross gives 3:1 phenotype ratio; dihybrid F2 cross gives 9:3:3:1 phenotype ratio. WAEC frequently asks you to complete a Punnett square or state the expected ratio.\n</div>\n",
