    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Variation</span> refers to the differences that exist among individuals of the same species. These differences are the raw material upon which evolution acts, and they arise from several distinct causes.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Variation</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Morphological</td><td>Differences in physical structure or appearance</td><td>Eye colour, height, fingerprint patterns</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Physiological</td><td>Differences in body function or metabolism</td><td>Blood group, enzyme activity, metabolic rate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Discontinuous</td><td>Traits that fall into distinct, separate categories with no intermediates</td><td>Blood group (A, B, AB, O), tongue rolling (can/cannot)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Continuous</td><td>Traits that vary gradually across a range with many intermediate values</td><td>Height, weight, skin colour</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Causes of Variation</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Mutation:</strong> Sudden, random changes in DNA that create new alleles</li>\n  <li><strong>Meiosis:</strong> The cell division process producing gametes shuffles genetic combinations through independent assortment and crossing over</li>\n  <li><strong>Fertilization:</strong> The random combination of genetic material from two parents during fertilization creates new genetic combinations in offspring</li>\n  <li><strong>Environment:</strong> External factors (nutrition, climate, exposure to sunlight) can influence how traits are expressed, though these changes are usually not inherited</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Adaptive Colouration, Mimicry, and Camouflage</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Camouflage</td><td>Colouring or pattern that helps an organism blend into its surroundings</td><td>Chameleons, stick insects</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mimicry</td><td>One species evolves to resemble another species, often a dangerous or unpalatable one, to avoid predation</td><td>Harmless hoverflies resembling stinging wasps</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Warning colouration</td><td>Bright colours that signal an organism is dangerous or toxic to potential predators</td><td>Poison dart frogs</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Discontinuous variation gives clear, countable categories (e.g., blood group), while continuous variation forms a smooth range best shown on a graph as a bell curve (e.g., height in a population).\n</div>\n",
