    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Evolution</span> is the gradual change in the inherited characteristics of populations over successive generations. This topic examines the major theories that attempt to explain how evolution occurs, and the evidence supporting it.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Lamarckism (Theory of Inheritance of Acquired Characteristics)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Jean-Baptiste Lamarck proposed that organisms could pass on characteristics acquired during their lifetime to their offspring. His classic example: giraffes stretching their necks to reach high leaves, and this \"stretched\" trait being passed to their young. This theory has been largely discredited, since acquired traits are not encoded in DNA and therefore cannot be inherited.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Darwinism (Theory of Natural Selection)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Charles Darwin proposed that evolution occurs through <span class=\"learn-keyword\">natural selection</span>: individuals with favourable inherited variations are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing those advantageous traits to the next generation. Over many generations, this leads to gradual change in the population. Unlike Lamarck, Darwin's theory relies on existing heritable variation, not traits acquired during an individual's life.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Modern Synthetic Theory of Evolution</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The modern synthetic theory combines Darwin's natural selection with Mendelian genetics and modern understanding of mutation, explaining how new genetic variation arises (mutation) and how natural selection acts on that variation to drive evolutionary change.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Evidence for Evolution</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Evidence Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fossil records</td><td>Preserved remains showing gradual changes in organisms over geological time</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Comparative anatomy</td><td>Similar skeletal structures in different species (e.g., homologous limb bones) suggesting a common ancestor</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Embryology</td><td>Similarities in the early embryonic development of different species, suggesting shared ancestry</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vestigial structures</td><td>Reduced, functionless body parts that were once useful in an ancestor (e.g., the human appendix)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Natural Selection and Adaptation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Natural selection favours individuals whose traits are best suited (adapted) to their environment. Over time, this process increases the frequency of advantageous traits within a population, improving its overall fitness for survival in that environment.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Don't confuse Lamarckism (acquired traits inherited) with Darwinism (existing heritable variation selected for). WAEC often asks you to identify which scientist proposed which theory.\n</div>\n",
