    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Human evolution</span> traces the biological and cultural development of modern humans (Homo sapiens) from earlier hominid ancestors, supported by fossil evidence spanning millions of years.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Origin of Man</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Scientific evidence indicates that modern humans share a common ancestry with other primates, particularly apes, and that the hominid lineage evolved in Africa over millions of years before spreading to other continents.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Key Stages in Human Fossil Evidence</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Key Features</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Australopithecus</td><td>Early hominid, walked upright (bipedal) but had a small brain, ape-like face; lived several million years ago in Africa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Homo habilis</td><td>\"Handy man\" — larger brain than Australopithecus, associated with early simple stone tool use</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Homo erectus</td><td>\"Upright man\" — larger brain, more advanced tools, controlled use of fire, first to migrate out of Africa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Homo sapiens</td><td>Modern humans — largest brain size, complex language, advanced tool use, art, and culture</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Similarities Between Humans and Apes</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Humans and apes (chimpanzees, gorillas) share many anatomical and genetic similarities, including similar skeletal structure, grasping hands, complex social behaviour, and a very high percentage of shared DNA — evidence of a common evolutionary ancestor.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Biological vs. Cultural Evolution</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Biological evolution:</strong> Physical changes in the human body over generations, such as increased brain size, upright posture, and changes in skeletal structure, driven by natural selection and genetics</li>\n  <li><strong>Cultural evolution:</strong> The development of tools, language, social organisation, art, and technology, passed on through learning rather than genes — allowing rapid adaptation without waiting for genetic change</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the general trend across hominid species: brain size and tool complexity generally increased over time from Australopithecus to Homo sapiens. WAEC often asks candidates to sequence these species correctly.\n</div>\n",
